!id: G2NmfdP4Ic
!front: What is the \( n \)-th Taylor polynomial of \( f \) centered at \( a \)?
!back:
If \( f \) is \( n \)-times differentiable at \( a \), its \( n \)-th Taylor polynomial centered at \( a \) is
\[ T_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k. \]
It is the polynomial of degree at most \( n \) whose derivatives at \( a \) match those of \( f \) through order \( n \).
~~~

!id: 9KI553nDBA
!front: What is the \( n \)-th Maclaurin polynomial of \( f \)?
!back:
The \( n \)-th Maclaurin polynomial is the Taylor polynomial centered at \( 0 \):
\[ T_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(0)}{k!}x^k. \]
So "Maclaurin" means the expansion point is \( a = 0 \).
~~~

!id: gHqQZYNvh5
!front: Why does the Taylor polynomial \( T_n \) match the derivatives of \( f \) at its center \( a \)?
!back:
For \( 0 \le j \le n \), differentiating
\[ T_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k \]
\( j \) times and then setting \( x=a \) kills every term except the \( k=j \) term. That remaining term equals \( f^{(j)}(a) \), so
\[ T_n^{(j)}(a)=f^{(j)}(a). \]
~~~

!id: g7jRPX2ABI
!front: Compare a Taylor polynomial and a Taylor series.
!back:
- A Taylor polynomial is a finite polynomial \( \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k \), so it is always defined once the needed derivatives at \( a \) exist.
- A Taylor series is the infinite series \( \sum_{k=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k \), so it raises separate questions of convergence and whether its sum equals \( f(x) \).
~~~

!id: FJyoiC4XFk
!front: State Taylor's theorem with Lagrange remainder.
!back:
If \( f \) has \( n+1 \) continuous derivatives on an interval containing \( a \) and \( x \), then
\[ f(x) = T_n(x) + R_n(x), \]
where
\[ T_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k \]
and for some \( c \) between \( a \) and \( x \),
\[ R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!}(x-a)^{n+1}. \]
~~~

!id: QVlID6zg9n
!front: How does a bound on \( f^{(n+1)} \) control the Taylor remainder?
!back:
If \( f \) has \( n+1 \) continuous derivatives on an interval containing \( a \) and \( x \), and \( |f^{(n+1)}(t)| \le M \) for every \( t \) between \( a \) and \( x \), then Taylor's theorem with Lagrange remainder gives
\[ |R_n(x)| \le \frac{M}{(n+1)!}|x-a|^{n+1}. \]
This turns derivative control on the interval into an explicit error bound for the Taylor approximation.
~~~

!id: 1I8Lis26B6
!front: When does the Taylor series of \( f \) centered at \( a \) actually represent \( f(x) \)?
!back:
Let \( R_n(x)=f(x)-T_n(x) \). The Taylor series represents \( f(x) \) exactly when the Taylor remainders tend to zero:
\[
  f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}\frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k
  \quad \Longleftrightarrow \quad
  \lim_{n\to\infty} R_n(x)=0.
\]
Convergence of the formal Taylor series by itself is not enough; its sum must converge to \( f(x) \), which is precisely what the vanishing remainder checks.
~~~

!id: Vk2ANvNfyh
!front: What does it mean for a real function \( f \) to be analytic at \( a \)?
!back:
A real function \( f \) is analytic at \( a \) if there is some \( r>0 \) and coefficients \( c_k \) such that
\[ f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty} c_k(x-a)^k \]
for every \( x \) with \( |x-a|<r \).

If \( f \) is represented this way, then \( c_k=\dfrac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} \), so the power series is its Taylor series.
~~~

!id: PHWnjJyBmD
!front: Why is a power series analytic inside its radius of convergence?
!back:
If
\[ f(x)=\sum_{k=0}^{\infty}c_k(x-a)^k \]
has radius of convergence \( R>0 \), then it represents \( f \) on \( |x-a|<R \). For any \( b \) with \( |b-a|<R \), choose \( r>0 \) such that \( |b-a|+r<R \). On \( |x-b|<r \), one can re-expand the same function as a convergent power series in powers of \( x-b \).

Thus \( f \) is analytic at every point \( b \) inside the interval of convergence.
!extra: At the original center \( a \), repeated termwise differentiation gives \( f^{(k)}(a)=k!c_k \), so the representing power series is exactly the Taylor series of \( f \) at \( a \).
~~~
